# Local-dev settings for the auth worker. Copy to `.dev.vars` (gitignored).
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# The client portal's own address — the SECOND front door. Google sign-in will
# only ever bounce someone back to one of these two origins.
PORTAL_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001
# "Continue with Google" (the `kwapso-signin` OAuth client). Leave both blank and
# the button simply isn't offered; the email-code path is unaffected. Whatever
# origins you set above must ALSO be registered in the Google console as
# <origin>/api/auth/google/callback.
#
# LOCAL DEV, HONESTLY: `next dev` REWRITES /api/auth/* to this worker on :8787,
# so the worker sees its own origin, not localhost:3000 — and the Google door
# refuses an origin it wasn't given (that refusal is the open-redirect defence,
# and it is not worth weakening for convenience). To exercise the round trip
# locally, run the GATEWAY with `wrangler dev` so screens and /api share one
# origin, and register that origin's callback with Google. Otherwise test Google
# on staging, which is what the redirect URIs are registered for anyway.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# (login codes are inbox-only — no echo var exists; STAGING dev/test sign-in uses
#  the test-login door on this worker, gated by its OWN TEST_LOGIN_KEY secret —
#  deliberately NOT ADMIN_KEY, so the maintenance key can never arm impersonation.
#  It fails closed when unset and refuses outright when ENVIRONMENT is production.)
TEST_LOGIN_KEY=set-on-staging-only-never-production
# ADMIN_KEY guards the maintenance endpoints only (migrate-teams, db-sizes, …).
ADMIN_KEY=
INSECURE_COOKIE=1
EMAIL_FROM=kwapso <onboarding@resend.dev>
# RESEND_API_KEY=
